#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500447} :activity/id #uuid "a5042924-7265-53a7-81d6-8c89c2f43133" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049557 :topic/id #uuid "fee59c68-0ad2-5141-b297-31cf8632cc46" :topic/math-academy-id 2167} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500394 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "faba9b8c-2db9-5102-aa65-00748558a463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367445} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d0ce5d-e84e-57e4-8a47-bf9cb06a4a06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367446} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1f8972-dbc3-5adb-b2ee-427452d50a48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367447} {:answer/id #uuid "8e3fff1f-5172-5721-9989-85b34c09b139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367448} {:answer/id #uuid "fb047e4f-69c3-5551-ba4d-0963ccacfe94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367449}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367447} :answer-field/id #uuid "778fca66-9171-578b-a82f-9dd5127d9503" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b9b18fd2-40c6-532b-a289-0305c371077f" :question/math-academy-id "q-137090" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $-4a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}54 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}54 & -18 & -9 \\\\ -18 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}54 & -18 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{9}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n-4a+\\frac{b}{c}=-4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6})+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3})}{1}=1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "29f94980-1925-529b-a3fe-5406bc761b87" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500421}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500405 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326897 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c8cf07-1252-5e91-b5c9-12f38c1f946f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324034} {:answer/id #uuid "91384dd4-5bcc-5860-814c-f96556460493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325204} {:answer/id #uuid "93d14029-007f-54bd-9d2a-0c82c9066807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325227} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f7e58b-5b63-5e1f-ae19-6232045969c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325419} {:answer/id #uuid "b3759c15-e036-5c30-848d-c2b42024912f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325509}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324034} :answer-field/id #uuid "6314ea6d-cf55-58df-9abc-6434d47d5c83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "db567127-1e8b-50cb-a3d2-dc5e2ea462cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-40950" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,+\\,{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $2a-3b-6c?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 & 12 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(1/6){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=(1/2){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable.  \n\nFrom the third row, we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{3}=1,\n$$\n\nand from the first row we obtain\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=1+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n2a-3b-6c=2(\\frac{1}{2})-3(1)-6(0)=-2.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "574a82d3-59e7-59b8-9908-4d23793ae75e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500449}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500421 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7360267e-e517-562f-a7ab-234dea8e16b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267158} {:answer/id #uuid "8a852bda-8769-55af-b974-271e085d782f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267380} {:answer/id #uuid "abc3d198-0e91-5133-9e31-d766dcdd7bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267694} {:answer/id #uuid "df227e4e-6f3a-5099-aa04-1ab50365aa3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268116} {:answer/id #uuid "f0784891-ea63-5109-abfd-c29f5a65f3e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268282}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267158} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fef7bda-8cd4-5d78-b705-1883723f2fd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7264a872-6f66-52f9-bc5f-7b2565f54aa2" :question/math-academy-id "q-137096" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a-\\frac{3b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 & 18 \\\\ 18 & 36\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 18 & -9 \\\\ 18 & 36 & -18\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 18 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-1-2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na-\\frac{3b}{c}=-1-\\frac{3(-2)}{1}=5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8a36cb6a-17f3-5f3e-b938-a84d2ec531d8"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500447 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134333 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2563641c-3674-599a-bc9b-6a7ff8dc6db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324278} {:answer/id #uuid "3380e44e-eb6f-5319-9afd-04efe1186590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324383} {:answer/id #uuid "4a8960b7-70fe-5198-ad4d-9a451b90165c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324576} {:answer/id #uuid "d91bf06c-7c91-517f-8dda-93fec52181f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325860} {:answer/id #uuid "f579c837-0e7b-5922-9481-0a119644e5dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326131}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325860} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6ad0704-99f4-509f-bb7d-ffd14144c5c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "871a1be1-8f4a-5616-a788-c400e98cd9b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-40943" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+2b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 12 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 12 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -8 & 12 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -8 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}+{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}-4(\\frac{3}{2}+{x}_{3})+2{x}_{3}=-2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2+{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2+{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+2b+c=1+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+0=4.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dfff254a-4249-53c2-9587-3cb4f4240ad9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500405}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500449 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c20af83-4b36-5128-89ee-a2b0f277470a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324458} {:answer/id #uuid "58d3b34f-653e-5908-8110-49345a2e51c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324708} {:answer/id #uuid "5c3eb528-5cf9-526f-857f-315ec81f0eb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324735} {:answer/id #uuid "b0bbe617-b13b-516f-806c-a9356a26279f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325479} {:answer/id #uuid "c5751e84-51f5-5505-bf51-a2e74963787c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325674}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325479} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac5ff18b-ee0a-5c8e-a1ac-a863dbac8c0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "85d7c223-d931-53ec-98fb-80a06e5b2764" :question/math-academy-id "q-41904" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $15a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}40 & -20 \\\\ -20 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -10 & 5 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}10 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n15a+\\frac{b}{c}=15\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{1}=2.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e72361cd-94e3-52fd-91cb-f7e78d6bf3a8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500394}}] :activity/title "The Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System (With Collinearity)" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499651}]}
